#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e57f9dab-c5db-55d9-9b21-375bbc92281c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6814" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151917 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "9abded65-4a6e-5ebc-b496-d61b28179288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6818" :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acf91a0-d644-5837-be02-ae7a75a73521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287284} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdefb26-6f9c-59e6-a300-b9ba36b0662e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287285} {:answer/id #uuid "414a4834-aebd-5766-a02f-e494225cf0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287286} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4b52f4-4953-5c88-bd2d-1636592e7089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287287} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d3a771-a99f-5f99-8677-9f9f7ab0151c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecdefb26-6f9c-59e6-a300-b9ba36b0662e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287285} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c5d9901-45ec-5a82-b5ff-6ae652bb348e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe4502b9-4c15-5777-ace8-7177e53571cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-106398" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/7b9f67594871385ccf7ac02c3af9e47a585116c162a08cdc1795d452a09912fe.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-3)$ with horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,-3),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "861da652-f677-5fa7-a40e-7ee0a493bbb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287291} {:answer/id #uuid "1f83c84e-621b-5000-b60c-556777725315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287292} {:answer/id #uuid "df45a23e-d530-5666-a794-d534c05bcf4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287293} {:answer/id #uuid "ba900f3f-81f4-5508-8e8f-09127ffabd6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/