$\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad88058-c875-5036-b4ea-3628069695fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287333} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb03b3c-e66b-5556-847f-39b8e859dc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287334} {:answer/id #uuid "432d428a-efcb-5ece-9091-69f2afa8dec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287335} {:answer/id #uuid "3c92de15-0997-5844-af75-b5727a650d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287336} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f59f1d-6c3d-54ad-867d-b18963ce5dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad88058-c875-5036-b4ea-3628069695fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287333} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad1b808d-be7f-533e-807d-3c989cce7046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86ea583d-a580-5671-b8bf-22431844e7be" :question/math-academy-id "q-15843" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf77ad8-e90f-5318-8684-5d94c505e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bf77ad8-e90f-5318-8684-5d94c505e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287340} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e675469-7ed3-50ca-b8c4-63856f23fd96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :